  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Scleroderma cepa, commonly known as the Onion Earthball, is a puffball-like fungus that develops underground or just at soil level before emerging to release spores. Despite its name, it is not edible and contains toxic compounds, making it important to distinguish from culinary onions.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Onion-like bulbous structure",
      "description": "The fruiting body is distinctly round and bulbous, resembling a small onion with a papery, tan to brown outer skin that often peels in layers",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Underground development",
      "description": "Typically forms below the soil surface before rupturing upward, often leaving a cup-like base embedded in soil",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Dark powdery interior",
      "description": "When mature, the interior reveals a dark, purple-black spore mass that becomes powdery when disturbed",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Onion Earthballs thrive in acidic, sandy to loamy soils in woodlands, mixed forests, and disturbed areas throughout New England, often fruiting in late summer through fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite resembling onions superficially, Scleroderma cepa is toxic and was historically used in folk medicine as a homeopathic remedy, though modern use is not recommended.",
